|segment[prx-unquestioned-once-actionable] :type probe :status fired :stage draft
  :tags [discounting verification]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md#appendix-a
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title What stopped being a question the moment it became actionable?

  |when
    Immediately after a plan forms, and again at the moment of acting on it.

  |act
    Look at the last thing you did and name what it *presupposed*. Then ask when
    each presupposition was checked. Usually the answer is never — because by the
    time the action formed, the presupposition had stopped presenting as a claim.

  |why
    Joseph's naming of the general symptom under every manifestation: *"the stunning
    frequency of things unquestioned once actionable"* (#exm-stunning-frequency).
    The promotion from uncertain to settled is silent and feels like nothing at all;
    it is the moment a hypothesis becomes a premise with nothing happening in
    between.

    Structurally this is the premise-facing half of #ver-discount-is-unquestioning,
    and it is separated from the document-facing half
    (#prx-what-did-i-decide-not-to-read) because the two fire at different moments
    even though they are one operation.

  |evidence
    **Fired, 2026-07-30, on its first run** alongside two sibling probes — three
    hits in under a minute, which is the datum that matters more than any one hit:
    the ambient density of unchecked inference is high enough that this is not a
    rare-catch instrument.

    **Failed to fire, same evening, in a successor instance**, which recommended a
    partition scheme for a fan-out built entirely on the presupposition that a
    search tool could filter by time and by scope. Neither was true. The
    presupposition was never checked because the plan required it — and it was
    caught only when the steward said the scoping flag "doesn't work very well."

  |working-notes
    That second entry is the more useful one and it argues for pairing this probe
    with an act: naming a presupposition is cheap and satisfying, and does not
    itself check anything. The version with teeth ends in a command run, not in a
    list produced.
